Subject: order() syntax question
Message-ID: <5B090EB78E8ADD4E9DFBD45E2240C2C2F0907B@MAIL2.joslin.harvard.edu>

dear List:

forgive me for asking such a simple question, but I am struggling with a small 
syntax problem.
I have a matrix with two columns ('a' and 'b') of information, and i'd like to 
sort ascending by the information in the first column, and then _descending_ by 
the information in the second column.

To sort by both columns _ascending_ I have successfully used:

ord <- order(mat[,"a"], mat[,"b"])
mat <- mat[ord,]

To do what I am trying to accomplish (sort by "a" ascending and "b" descending) 
I tried:

ord <- order(mat[,"a", rev(mat[,"b"]))
mat <- mat[ord,]

but this doesn't seem to work. I think I am using rev() in the wrong manner, 
but am not sure.
I'd much appreciate any suggestions.

with thanks,

Jonathan

From andy_liaw at merck.com  Sun May  9 01:41:54 2004
From: andy_liaw at merck.com (Liaw, Andy)
Date: Sat, 08 May 2004 21:41:54 -0400
Subject: Re: order() syntax question
In-Reply-To: <5B090EB78E8ADD4E9DFBD45E2240C2C2F0907B@MAIL2.joslin.harvard.edu>
Message-ID: <3A822319EB35174CA3714066D590DCD504AF7D51@usrymx25.merck.com>

What you want is probably something like:

mat[order(mat[,"a"], -mat[,"b"]),]

order() is sorting the vector, so reversing the order _before_ doesn't help.
If you just want to order by column "b", then mat[rev(order(mat[,"b"])),]
would have worked; i.e., you want to reverse the output of order(), not the
input.

HTH,
Andy

