From: spencer.graves at pdf.com (Spencer Graves)
Date: Tue, 11 May 2004 09:29:22 -0700
Subject: Re: Is there an easy way to test whether slopes of a linear correlation
In-Reply-To: <1644C21C399A7B4EBFD7E5224209EFE361B592@EXCHG-VS1.risoe.dk>
References: <1644C21C399A7B4EBFD7E5224209EFE361B592@EXCHG-VS1.risoe.dk>
Message-ID: <40A0FF62.7090907@pdf.com>

Thanks for the question.  Using the interaction would be a more 
appropriate response to your question.  The "x+factor(z)" formulation 
assumes that the correlation is the same for different levels of z, but 
it might be different overall due to mixing subpopulations with 
different means.  For what you want, you should group the interaction 
with the main effect of z in the anova as follows:

> set.seed(1)
> DF <- data.frame(x = 1:12, z = rep(letters[1:3], 4), y = rnorm(12))
> fit0 <- lm(y ~ x, DF)
> fitz <- lm(y ~ x * z, DF)
> anova(fit0, fitz)
Analysis of Variance Table

Response: y

  Terms Resid. Df      RSS   Test Df Sum of Sq   F Value     Pr(F)
1     x        10 9.234301
2 x * z         6 7.844045 +z+x:z  4  1.390257 0.2658559 0.8897605

hope this helps.  spencer graves
