From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Wed, 12 May 2004 19:09:04 +0100
Subject: Re: comparing models fitted with glmmPQL
In-Reply-To: <NEBBJPNMELJBDMHCFGOFIEKJDCAA.David.Coomes@plantsci.cam.ac.uk>
References: <NEBBJPNMELJBDMHCFGOFIEKJDCAA.David.Coomes@plantsci.cam.ac.uk>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.44.0405121904520.17543-100000@gannet.stats>

Unfortunately, to use AIC you need to have maximized a likelihood, and PQL 
does not do that.

On Wed, 12 May 2004, David Coomes wrote:

> I'm fitting a series of mixed-effects models with Poisson errors using
> glmmPQL (in the MASS4 library), using Splus6.0.  I have several candidate
> models which I wish to compare, which differ in their fixed effects, but not
> their random effects.  Can I do this by comparing their AIC values?

If they are nested, then you can do approximate Wald tests based on the 
standard errors.  One day we do hope to have reasonable approximate ML 
fits for this problem, but the algorithmic details are extremely tricky, 
much trickier than we ever expected.

