It's completely conventional usage. On p.159 of the current edition of 
McC & N.

> To understand how multinom() works, I used the example on prevalence of
> pneumoconiosis (normal, category2 and severe) predicted by period of
> exposure of coalminers provided by McCullagh & Nelder (1983) Generalized
> Linear Models, Table 5.2.

McCullagh and Nelder do not fit (at that point) a multinomial logistic
regression, so you cannot get their results from multinom. Perhaps you
first need to understand what a multinomial logistic regression is?

Since normal is the base category, you want category to have levels
normal, category2, severe, *in that order*.

> > my.model <- multinom (category ~ log(period), weights = n)
> > my.model
> Call:
